<details id="reading" class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="reading-title"><summary class="reading-head"><h2 id="reading-title">Place details</h2><span id="reading-context" class="small">Select a place on the map.</span></summary>
<div class="reading-grid"><div><div id="history-answer"><p>Select a building or a point on the map to read its history and sources.</p></div><div id="source-readings"></div></div><aside><h3>Evidence, inference, absence</h3><div id="reading-gaps"><p>Explore each source’s date, viewpoint and connection to this place.</p></div><p class="small">Generated scenes are interpretations, not historical photographs. Sources and uncertain details remain visible.</p></aside></div></details><details id="question-panel"><summary class="question-panel-head"><strong>Ask about the evidence</strong><span class="question-panel-hint">Source-based historical questions</span></summary><form id="question-form" class="composer"><div class="composer-top"><label id="prompt-label" for="question">What would you like to know about this place?</label><select id="prompt-mode" aria-label="Purpose of your text"><option value="history">Explore history</option></select></div><p id="question-context" class="small" aria-live="polite">Questions use the point selected on the map.</p><div class="composer-row"><textarea id="question" maxlength="500" rows="2" placeholder="What did this place look like in 1935?" required></textarea><button id="ask" type="submit" class="button" disabled>Ask this place</button></div><p id="generation-message" class="messages" role="status"></p><p id="question-message" class="messages" role="status"></p><div class="utility"><span id="location-coverage">Loading archive locations…</span><details><summary>Availability & saved study</summary><p id="generation-allowance">Checking generation availability…</p><p id="view-mode"></p><div id="model-adjust" class="downloads" hidden><a id="download-model" download="speculative-model.glb">Download GLB</a><a id="saved-model" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open saved study ↗</a></div><a id="download-render" hidden download="speculative-reconstruction.png">Download speculative photo</a></details></div></form><details id="references-panel" class="contextual"><summary id="references-summary">Photographic references for the next reconstruction</summary><p class="small">The selected building photo is always included. Up to three nearby images can supply additional context. Different source dates remain distinct.</p><div id="reference-list" class="reference-list"></div></details></details></section>
<section id="provenance-section" class="provenance" hidden aria-labelledby="provenance-title"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence and authorship</p><h2 id="provenance-title">How this photograph was made</h2><div class="provenance-grid"><div><img id="capture-preview" alt="Confirmed 3D viewpoint supplied to image generation"><p class="small">Confirmed camera reference</p></div><div id="render-provenance"></div></div></section>

<section class="archive-links" aria-label="Continue into the archive"><div><a id="context-archive" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sources for this place and year ↗</a><p id="archive-context-note">Follow the evidence for your map selection in a separate archive page.</p></div><div><a id="all-archive" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse the archive ↗</a><p>Explore the full collection, including photographs and recollections whose precise locations remain unresolved.</p></div></section>


<div class="resource-notes74"><details id="project-context" class="project-context"><summary>About After Chinatown</summary><h2>Remembering everyday life in a changing city.</h2><p>Urban change can erase familiar places while memories live on. After Chinatown preserves the traces of Chinese American life in Detroit: its gathering places, family stories and everyday experiences.</p><p>Many of these experiences remain scattered or absent from public records. Keeping them accessible gives future generations a connection to the city their families knew.</p><p class="credit-note">Historical materials are credited in their individual source records. Spatial references include the Library of Congress Sanborn Maps, the City of Detroit Chinatown StoryMap, and Detroit historical archives.</p></details><details id="method" class="method"><summary>Map notes &amp; sources</summary><p>Dots mark major milestones. Every year remains selectable. Earlier records remain in the archive.</p><p>Buildings follow the selected map and recorded changes. White and pink volumes remain opaque between surveys; colour does not establish uninterrupted survival. Inventory reference forms may be conjectural. Select a building to inspect its evidence.</p><p>Filter photographs by year or date range. Include undated images to explore sources without a confirmed date.</p><p>Dark blue marks a route documented through the present; light blue includes earlier or unresolved routes. Road records can describe legal changes without establishing physical opening or removal dates.</p><div id="map-evidence" class="map-evidence"></div><details id="road-evidence" class="road-evidence"><summary>Road sources</summary><div id="road-source-list"></div></details><p id="semcog-credit" class="small" data-no-translate>Copyright © 2024 SEMCOG. All Rights Reserved. Reproduction or Use Without Permission is Prohibited. <a href="https://maps-semcog.opendata.arcgis.com/pages/copyright-license-agreement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">License</a></p><div id="layer-method"><p>Historical images share the building coordinate system and retain their source year. Between surveys, the last mapped form may remain as a reference; this does not prove that every building was unchanged. Inventory backcasts retain their uncertainty in the building record. Yellow identifies a documented total building life over 50 years; dated Chinatown associations take pink priority.</p><p>Community-label intensity is a qualitative reading of cited activity, not population data. Labels move with the 1963 relocation; fewer businesses do not mean the community disappeared. The kiosk is a separate layer, with an approximate 1960s installation date.</p><p>Railway service, construction and removal dates are distinct. Historical tracings are combined with municipal and regional reference alignments. Elevated rail and the Ford–Lodge crossing use illustrative heights.</p><p><a href="https://www.thepeoplemover.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">People Mover history ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=540e3a12ec24427ebc4f8fa3f88368f7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rail alignment source ↗</a> ·  · </p></div><div id="area-methods"></div></details></div>
</main><footer class="credits"><div class="credits-inner"><div><p class="authors">Designed and developed by<br><strong><a href="https://teng-teng.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Teng Teng</a> and Zhen Cheng</strong></p></div><div></div></div><div class="build"><span>Detroit Chinatown / Research prototype</span><span id="build-label"></span></div></footer>

